Preserve missing, censored and invalid observations (@cm-monitoring-missing: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN", #clean-manufacturing "Investigate-a-signal-with-competing-physical-explanations.lingua#r_1DBCZNKZTTP4TG3YP0A1CS17A8") { r_37V4MMGQK1MWAAJ4QM2HF58YE7
**Read:** [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), model diagnostics and data analysis; revisit detection limits and the measurement chain.

A missing interval, a value below a reporting limit, an overloaded detector and a valid zero are different records of evidence. Replacing all four with zero can manufacture an apparent improvement. A missingness mechanism related to the process, such as loss of readings during high loading, biases the observed history.

Suppose 80 of 100 intended observations average 1, and the 20 missing values are known only to lie between 0 and 10. The complete mean can range from 0.8 to 2.8. The observed mean alone does not choose within this interval. This is a deterministic bound under stated limits, not a confidence interval.

Keep validity flags, collection duration, units, processing versions and reasons for exclusion. A later corrected result should remain connected to the earlier value and the evidence supporting the correction. Calibration history is part of interpreting a long record, not an annotation unrelated to the numbers.

**Work:** Introduce censoring, overload and state-dependent gaps into a simulated monitoring history. Compare naive substitution, bounded statements and an explicit observation model. Identify conclusions that survive the missing information and those requiring additional evidence.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
